[{:seon.task/name "branches-as-in-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-21: \"I want us using branches like we will in production. separate stores should be a last ditch effort where we are backing up and really trying to debug something.\" Production is one store and many branches; tests, checks and runs work the same way. Today every place makes its own database: 30 c/start! in seon.check and 6 in seon.e2e (each a fresh LMDB store seeded with 2,000 or 300 datoms: ~0.45 s each, open alone 213 ms where an mmap and a schema tx should cost a few ms; find out why), ~170 MB kept per bin/check run and unbounded across runs; and five unit-test files each carrying their own copy of a fresh in-memory db helper (merge_test, program_test, context_test, task_cost_test, program_cost_test; plus seon.db's memory cfg at db.clj:40). Make it one store: bin/check seeds one store per run and each step's root works on its own branch of it (branch! from the seeded commit); a step that proves recovery (durable, window, resume) kills and reopens that same store, as production would; bin/test makes one seeded test store and each test takes a branch of it, through one helper (the five copies go). The T5 harness and bin/root run tasks as branches of the one kept store. A separate store is a last resort, for a backup or a deep debugging session, and says why where it is made. Estimate first: a branch should cost about one commit (sub-ms) against ~450 ms per boot; disk per check run should be the seed plus each step's own writes. Measure both and put them in the commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/steps-are-branches-of-one-store seon.db-test/a-test-takes-a-branch-of-the-test-store}}]
